# ~/.config/zsh/secrets.zsh - machine-local credentials
#
# TEMPLATE. Copy to ~/.config/zsh/secrets.zsh, fill in the real values, then:
#
#     chmod 600 ~/.config/zsh/secrets.zsh
#
# copy.sh does that for you on a fresh machine: it creates secrets.zsh from
# this file if - and only if - one does not already exist, so re-running the
# installer can never overwrite keys you have already filled in.
#
# Why this file is not in the repo
# --------------------------------
# It holds live credentials. Anything committed to git is recoverable from the
# history forever, even after a later commit deletes it, and this repo is
# pushed to GitHub. So the real secrets.zsh stays out of version control and
# only this example - with fake values - is tracked.
#
# How it gets loaded
# ------------------
# .zshrc sources it, guarded:
#
#     [ -r "$HOME/.config/zsh/secrets.zsh" ] && . "$HOME/.config/zsh/secrets.zsh"
#
# The guard means a shell on a machine without this file still starts normally.
# It just will not have the keys, so only the tools that need one will fail.
#
# Values are per machine, so on a new install these have to be recreated by
# hand from your password manager. Nothing in this repo can restore them.

# ── Anthropic ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only needed for direct API use: SDK scripts, curl, anything hitting the
# Messages API. Claude Code itself does NOT read this - it authenticates by
# OAuth and keeps its own token in ~/.claude/.credentials.json.
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="sk-ant-1234"

# ── Cloudflare ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# export CLOUDFLARE_EMAIL="you@example.com"
# export CLOUDFLARE_API_KEY="replace-me"

# ── Bitbucket ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# export BITBUCKET_TOKEN="replace-me"

# Add whatever else this machine needs below. Keep one export per line so the
# file stays greppable, and keep the mode at 600 - it is readable by your user
# only, and zsh does not care about the mode but you should.
